Q: Unity crashes after I run my save file script A: <p>As far as I can tell there are various issues here:</p>
<ul>
<li><p>First of all it makes no sense to try and plain out serialize a <code>GameObject</code> to JSON.</p>
<p>look alone at <code>GameObject.transform</code> &lt;-&gt; <code>Transform.gameObject</code> and <code>GameObject</code> has a ton of such properties that can go very deep nested loops. it also has legacy properties like e.g. <code>renderer</code>, <code>camera</code>, <code>collider</code>, <code>rigidbody</code> etc. They are obsolete but exist</p>
<p>you are basically trying to force it to deep serialize entire prefab code just in bad.</p>
<p>Anyway, what exactly is a <code>GameObject</code> supposed to look like in JSON? I'm pretty sure this is not what you want at all.</p>
<p>What you can do is e.g. check for attached components, have a deep serialization and later when loading that save again create them one by one. Not sure if this is what you really want and need here.</p>
<p>To me it sounds like some sort of &quot;serialize all balls of certain type&quot; thing to me. So the solution here would be simply store just that type and when loading create them accordingly.</p>
</li>
<li><p>and then it is known that <code>Vector3</code> doesn't really play well with Newtonsoft JSON because it contains some properties which again return <code>Vector</code> themselves (e.g. <code>normalized</code>) and Newtonsoft tries to serialize them all basically ending in an infinite serialization loop. They are structs and never the same reference!</p>
<p><code>ReferenceLoopHandling.Ignore</code> is for ignoring when you actually encounter the same object instance reference. Which is e.g. useful for some graph where a circle reference is valid. It has nothing to do with your data structure.</p>
<p>And then also there are also other properties which return <code>float</code> and you will still serialize them (<code>magnitude</code> etc)</p>
<p>For this you usually introduce your own custom mirror type which only is a plain x,y,z without any further fancyness and girth and back convert between them for the serialization.</p>
</li>
<li><p>then also - depending on whether this is at any point supposed to be running outside of the UnityEditor but rather in a build - in a build you can't simply write to <code>Directory.GetCurrentDirectory()</code> on most platforms. Rather check out e.g. <a href="https://docs.unity3d.com/6000.3/Documentation/ScriptReference/Application-persistentDataPath.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>Application.persistentDataPath</code></a></p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>Besides these</p>
<ul>
<li>when using <code>Rigidbody</code> you can just use its <code>position</code> directly</li>
<li>when going through <code>Transform</code> then there is no reason to use <code>GetComponent</code> to get it</li>
</ul>
<p>but that's rather minor side notes compared to the above</p>
<hr />
<p>So skipping the <code>GameObject</code> for now I would have something like e.g.</p>
<pre><code>public class SerializeVector3
{
    public float x, y, z;

    public static implicit operator Vector3(SerializeVector3 vec) =&gt; new Vector3(vec.x, vec.y, vec.z);

    public static implicit operator SerializeVector3(Vector3 vec) =&gt; new SerializeVector3 { x = vec.x, y = vec.y, z = vec.z };
}

public class SaveFile 
{   
    // Skip until you actually know what exactly you want to save here 
    // public List&lt;GameObject&gt; SAVED_GB = new ();
    public List&lt;SerializeVector3&gt; SAVED_VEL = new ();
    public List&lt;SerializeVector3&gt; SAVED_TRANS = new ();
}
</code></pre>
<p>and then</p>
<pre><code>    public void OnSave()
    {   
        //Saver.SAVED_GB.Clear();
        Saver.SAVED_VEL.Clear();
        Saver.SAVED_TRANS.Clear();

        var componentRBs = GameObject.FindObjectsByType&lt;Rigidbody&gt;(FindObjectsSortMode.None);

        foreach (var rb in componentRBs)
        {
            if (rb.gameObject.CompareTag(&quot;Player&quot;)) continue;

            Saver.SAVED_VEL.Add(rb.linearVelocity);
            //Saver.SAVED_GB.Add(rb.gameObject);
            Saver.SAVED_TRANS.Add(rb.position); // implicitly converts to SerializeVector3 

        }   

        string json = JsonConvert.SerializeObject(Saver, Formatting.Indented, new JsonSerializerSettings { ReferenceLoopHandling = ReferenceLoopHandling.Ignore});
        File.WriteAllText(path, json);  // already overwrites file if it already exists       
    }
</code></pre>

Q: Change Color of Entry Underline or Remove this Color A: <p>in Platform for Android, needs to add the handler, looks like this:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>protected override MauiApp CreateMauiApp()
{
    // Remove Entry control underline
    Microsoft.Maui.Handlers.EntryHandler.Mapper.AppendToMapping(&quot;NoUnderline&quot;, (h, v) =&gt;
    {
        h.PlatformView.BackgroundTintList =
            Android.Content.Res.ColorStateList.ValueOf(Colors.Transparent.ToAndroid());
    });

    return MauiProgram.CreateMauiApp();
}
</code></pre>

Q: EditText underline below text property A: <p>You can change the color of EditText programmatically. Just use:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>edittext.setBackgroundTintList(ColorStateList.valueOf(yourcolor));
</code></pre>
